[Music]  hello everyone its Alicia Malone welcome  to Fandango indie movie Guide coming to  you from the Toronto International Film  Festival  now the films that I mentioned that I  was looking forward to last week did  bigger films like mother the shape of  water battle of the sexes and three  billboards outside Irving Missouri have  all been at the top of my list of  favorites here at TIFF but what about  some of the smaller movies he should put  on your radar  [Music]  let's start with bodied which on the  surface is about rap battles but  underneath it's about racism privilege  fait quirkiness and who gets to say what  under the guise of entertainment it's by  Joseph Kahn a well known music video  director in fact he just did the video  clip for Taylor Swift's look what you  made me do and so he brings that unique  eye that visual energy to this movie  which has a lot to say and is so much  fun to watch  Velma is a Norwegian thriller by  director Yocum tria it's getting a lot  of comparisons to Stephen King's Carrie  and for good reason it's about a young  girl who suffers from powerful seizures  which unearth dangerous superpowers and  she is scared of herself and her own  feminine power it's intriguing it's  unsettling it really takes its time to  reveal and it's one that will stick in  your head for a long time afterwards 1%  is an Australian crime thriller that's  been described as Macbeth on motorbikes  it has a very Shakespearean seeming  story all about loyalty between real  family and loyalty between created  family in this case a bikie gang which  goes to war between the President and  the Vice President after the vice  president's brother gets in trouble  it is exciting it's violent its crude it  will not be for everyone but I really  enjoyed the cast I enjoyed the story and  I was engaged the whole time I feel like  it's something new and exciting from my  own home country of Australia  [Music]  you should also look out for a fantastic  woman which is a Chilean drama all about  a trans woman who has to deal with love  grief after suddenly losing her older  lover she also has to deal with a lot of  disdain and disrespect from the other  people involved in his life including  the police doctors but especially his  family it gives you a small insight into  the type of everyday obstacles that  trans people may have to overcome and  it's got a brilliant performance by the  lead actress Daniela  Vega call me by your name which is  directed by Luca Guadagnino based on a  book of the same name all about two guys  of the 1980s sometime somewhere in  northern Italy fall in love over summer  it is sensual it is tender it is  sensitive and completely heartbreaking I  saw it for a second time here it stars  armie hammer and Timothy Salome and it  is very very well done it's still one of  my favorite films of 2017 and the  florida project directed by Sean Baker  this film I caught in can and it was one  of my favorites there continues again to  stay at the top of my list of 2017 it's  all about the invisible homeless living  in Florida living just on the outskirts  of Disneyland the stories all told  through a child's perspective so even  though they're struggling with poverty  they don't quite realize it to them  these hotels are their own magical  kingdom it is very well done and very  well active particularly by the young  kids  and that's it for me from the Toronto  International Film Festival the sun's  coming out it is warming up so that  means I need to get back inside into the  dark and watch some more movies I'm  Alicia Malone you can follow me on  twitter at Alicia Malone and I'll see  you soon  [Music]

